# Normalize line endings so a checkout is identical on every platform.
#
# This is not cosmetic, and on this project it is load-bearing.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true at the system level by default. That
# rewrites LF to CRLF in the working copy for every file .gitattributes does not
# pin. The direct consequence here is that internal/report/testdata/golden/*.txt
# and *.json would be checked out with CRLF and compared BYTE-EXACTLY by
# TestGoldenTerminal and friends, so every golden test would fail on Windows
# while passing on Linux. A failure that depends only on the contributor's
# operating system is the worst kind of test failure: it looks like a code
# problem and is not one.
#
# The rules below do two things:
#   * text=auto  normalizes to LF in the repository, so no CRLF is ever committed
#   * eol=lf     checks out with LF everywhere, including Windows
#
# text=auto means git decides whether a file is text, so binaries are untouched.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts that users pipe to bash or that the release pipeline runs.
#
# Restated explicitly for documentation value rather than necessity: the default
# above already covers them. A shebang of `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` points at a
# path that does not exist, and bash reports `$'\r': command not found`, which
# is confusing enough that the failure deserves a comment next to the rule that
# prevents it.
*.sh text eol=lf
scripts/** text eol=lf

# Configuration and workflow definitions. YAML with CRLF parses, but a value
# that ends in CR becomes part of the string, which silently breaks the string
# comparisons in the config loader and the workflow analyzer.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# The release spec is read by a Go program, so bytes matter.
.goreleaser.yaml text eol=lf

# Golden report fixtures. Pinned separately because these are compared byte for
# byte, and because a silent line-ending change here would look like a layout
# regression in a twelve-file diff.
internal/report/testdata/** text eol=lf

# Generated or machine-managed; the diff is noise and the merge is a conflict.
go.sum -diff
dashboard/package-lock.json -diff

# Binary and vendored content: never touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
# The launch video linked from the README. Declared binary for the same reason
# as the images above: `* text=auto eol=lf` below lets git classify files itself,
# and a file it guesses wrong about is a file whose bytes change on someone
# else's checkout.
*.mp4 binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.exe binary
*.db binary

# The embedded dashboard is copied in by scripts/prepare-release.sh and is not
# committed, but declare it binary so a local rebuild cannot corrupt the index.
internal/server/assets/** binary